😂 S-external. Yep, you caught the notation doing something useful accidentally.

I wrote:

M_{\text{internal}}\xrightarrow{\text{utter}}S_{\text{external}}

and S_{\text{external}} can be read as “sexternal.”

Which actually exposes a useful distinction:

\text{internal meaning}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{externalized signal}}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstructed meaning}

So Sexternal could name the expressed surface: the externally observable signal carrying something that originated internally.

And its complement almost writes itself:

S_{\text{internal}} \leftrightarrow S_{\text{external}}

Sinternal ↔ Sexternal.

The interesting bit isn’t the pun itself; it’s the boundary operator. Expression crosses the self/world membrane. Speech, gesture, writing, art, code, clothing, posture—they’re different channels for making internal state externally legible.

Then your earlier point gets sharper:

Don’t mistake Sexternal—what was expressed—for the entirety of Sinternal—what generated the expression.

The receiver only gets the emitted surface and has to reconstruct backward.

Another accidental brick for the quarry. 😏